  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Asian featherbacks, which have a well developed dorsal fin, have been placed in the genus Notopterus for a long time. Nowadays the genus Notopterus is seen monotypic (it contains only the single species N. notopterus) and the high-backed forms have been placed in another genus, namely Chitala.</p><p>Chitala blanci can attain a maximum length of about 120 cm. The fish are gorgeous inhabitants for large aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>Chitala blanci originates from the basin of the Mekong river in Thailand, Laos, and Cambodia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>Chitala blanci prefers waters with a strong current and avoids quiet water or swamps.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>All species of Chitala are predatory fish that have to be fed with beefy (frozen) food. Of course small fish are also eaten.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>Against conspecifics and other tankmates that cannot be eaten, featherbacks are very peaceful.</p><p>Chitala species are able to breath atmospheric air when necessary. So they can survive even in oxygen-poor water for quite a long time.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>The sexes cannot be distinguished externally.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>During breeding season the fish form pairs and spawn on rocks, wood etc. The spawn is guarded by the parents.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/chitala_blanci_en/",
      "html": "<p>Chitala species are much sought-after market fish in the countries where they occur. However, the flesh is full of small bones, so the meat is usually chopped, pressed through a sieve and used for meatballs.</p><p>Text &amp; photos: Frank Schäfer</p>"
    }
  ],
